{"authored" [{"question" "q-125085" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)={e}^{5x}$ about $x=0$ is" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039968/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7832fb9d9184d948bcd10812d1b20d5298ef6ac434d7eccf2d7a08cad0de808" "basis" 1397 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039968/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-125085" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the second-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=0,$ we plug $a=0$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(0)+{f}^{′}(0)(x-0)+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(0)(x-0)^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to work out $f(0),$ ${f}^{′}(0),$ and ${f}^{″}(0):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{5x} & ⟹ &  & f(0) & =1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =5{e}^{5x} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{′}(0) & =5 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =25{e}^{5x} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{″}(0) & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now plug the results for $f(0),$ ${f}^{′}(0),$ and ${f}^{″}(0)$ into our second-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =1+5x+\\frac{25}{2}{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039968/state.json" "file_sha256" "c7832fb9d9184d948bcd10812d1b20d5298ef6ac434d7eccf2d7a08cad0de808" "basis" 1397 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039968/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-125085" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14039968/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bb9ac91e616c0d2ddac5311af2286afb0946a05b6be